A('<h1>One ball: the tree, the gap and the overlap</h1><p class="small">14 November 2026, version 1. Working notes by Claude for Jeromie Beasley, from a second pass over Two Trees in One Ball v4, the Knot Atlas Commutator Eye v3, One Tree One Ball, Two Trees One Scale, VOTKP Mixer v2, BallJoins.lean and the atom turn-book bridge page. Grades: <span class="g">in his files</span> (paper, Lean or mixer already states it); <span class="g">ran</span> (checked here, line written first); <span class="o">recognised</span> (standard geometry, not looked up today); <span class="r">open</span>. AI-assisted.</p>')
A('<h2>1. The question, and where his own files already answer it</h2><p>The question of 12 November: push another ball right up to one, let them connect at certain levels but always leave a layer of gap; these should be the angles and connections of a crystal lattice. On 14 November: there is usually some overlap on an outer or inner layer, for the push and the pull, the holding and the excluding.</p>')
A('<p>In his files a second ball is not a second sphere. It is the next node of the tree, one edge of length ln z away, and the tree looks the same from every node (One Tree One Ball). Each ball then owns the region nearer to it than to any neighbour. Its walls are the planes halfway to each neighbour, at ln z / 2 along each arm. These are the planes the paper uses in Theorem 8.4, and the Commutator Eye draws the same construction as caps.</p>')
A('<table><tr><th>His words</th><th>What it is in the one ball</th><th>Where</th></tr>'
'<tr><td>always leave a layer of gap</td><td>Past the wall the walls of neighbouring balls do not meet. Along a straight step they are ln z apart; across a turn they are δ apart, with cosh δ = sinh²(ln z / 2) = q. At z = 7, cosh δ = 9/7.</td><td class="g">paper Thm 8.4; general q ran here</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>some overlap</td><td>Inside the wall the walls of two perpendicular neighbours cross, at angle α with cos α = q. At z = 5, α = 36.87°. The Commutator Eye shows this as the step image spilling out of its cap.</td><td class="g">paper Fig. 3, Thm 5.3; angle ran here</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>the holding</td><td>Inside the wall the square of four steps is a turn about a fixed point; its powers never go farther than 3.5255 from the centre at z = 5. The walk is held.</td><td class="g">paper Thm 5.4; Eye readout</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>the excluding</td><td>Past the wall each step carries everything outside the opposite cap into its own cap. Nothing comes back, the group is discrete, the walk runs to the rim.</td><td class="g">paper Thm 5.3; PingPong7.lean</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>angles and connections of the lattice</td><td>Section 2 below.</td><td class="g">ran here</td></tr></table>')
A('<h2>2. What was checked today, in his placement</h2><p>Script <code>cell_check_01.py</code>, six boosts of length ln z along ±i, ±j, ±k acting on the centre, exactly Definition 4.1. Four lines were written before the run; none was hit.</p>')
A('<img src="data:image/png;base64,'+img+'" alt="Overlap angle inside the wall and gap layer past it">')
A('<table><tr><th>Line</th><th>Result</th></tr>'
'<tr><td>C1. Walls of perpendicular neighbours cross at α, cos α = q, or are δ apart, cosh δ = q, with q = (z − 1)²/(4z)</td><td class="g">Holds to 1e-16 at every z tried (2, 3, 5, 7, 11 and the special steps).</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>C2. The square of four steps turns by 4α inside the wall and runs by 4δ past it</td><td class="g">Holds. At z = 5 it gives cos φ = −527/625 (paper), φ = 147.48° (the Eye). At z = 7 it gives cosh τ = 23137/2401 (paper). The turn 212.52° in the balls mixer v1 is the same turn counted the other way round.</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>C3. Where α = 360°/n the square has order n exactly</td><td class="g">Holds for n = 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12 (z = 2.8901, 3.7321, 4.2592, 4.6116, 5.0376, 5.2745).</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>C4. At those steps the whole six-arm tree closes into a lattice: the smallest distance between distinct balls stays ln z as the tree grows</td><td class="g">Holds to depth 5 for n = 5, 6, 8 (2,643, 3,667 and 4,555 distinct balls; smallest distance 1.06128, 1.31696, 1.52857 = ln z each time). Controls: at z = 3 it falls to 0.0233 by depth 5; at z = 5 it falls to 0.4258 at depth 5, the paper\'s own number. <span class="r">n = 7, 10, 12 not run for C4; depth 6 not measured.</span></td></tr></table>')
A('<p>So in the one ball, with six arms: inside the wall the neighbours overlap, and the tree is a crystal exactly when a whole number n of balls fit round each edge, cos(360°/n) = q. Between those steps the balls never settle (free, not discrete). At the wall the walls touch at the rim. Past it there is a gap layer, nothing closes and every word is free. One number q = R/T of the step carries all of it; BallJoins J4 is the same q.</p>')
A('<p class="small">Recognised, not looked up today: the n = 5 and n = 6 cases are the two cubic honeycombs of hyperbolic space (five and six cubes round an edge; the second has its corners on the rim). What is his is reaching them from the peel step: the six-arm tree of ratio z is that honeycomb exactly at z = 2.8901, where cosh ln z is the golden ratio, and at z = 2 + √3, where Γ² = 1/3. The whole-number steps 5 and 7 are not closing steps: 5 sits between n = 8 and n = 10 (α = 36.87°), 7 is past the wall.</p>')
A('<h2>3. What each file holds that bears on this</h2><table><tr><th>File</th><th>Holds</th></tr>'
'<tr><td>Two Trees in One Ball v4 (body read in full again; the two HTML copies are identical and the PDF is the same paper, 101 pages; appendices not re-read)</td><td>Placement (Def 4.1); straight adds, turn shortens (4.3); Thomas turn tan(θ/2) = Γ² with triple (65, 72, 97) at 5 (4.6, 4.7); no word returns (5.1, 5.2, 8.1); the line Γ² = 1/2 as Schottky threshold (5.3); free but not discrete below it, fixed point (3, 2, 2, 0) (5.4); doubled steps are always clean (ln 25 is past the line); constants ℓ and δ at ln 7 (8.4); knot meridians as steps of 2x, the commutator band and the same line (8.6); seven knots never turn (8.8).</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>Knot Atlas 3D, Commutator Eye v3 (page text and the Eye code read in full; other five views by their outline only)</td><td>The square and the gap it leaves; orbit of its powers; fixed point; caps and their images; doubled-step tree; residue colours; the knot\'s commutator on the same disk. Peel ratio slider with z = 3, 5, the line, 7, 25.</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>One Tree One Ball</td><td>One ball; the camera stands on a ball and the tree slides by one peel step; the same tree from every ball; level shells round whichever ball is the centre.</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>Two Trees One Scale</td><td>Every ball carries a six-arm star turned by its word\'s frame; height ln n as the shared scale. Its text cites a BallJoins J10 that the BallJoins file here does not contain (it stops at J8).</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>BallJoins.lean</td><td>J1/J8 a ratio of two counts is a peel step; J2 the rung ceilings 1/(2k + 1) are steps of ratio (k + 1)/k; J3 the line is 3 + 2√2; J4 the square\'s trace is 2 − 4(R/T)²; J5 the turn triple; J7 reverse order, reverse turn.</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>Atom turn-book bridge</td><td>Two line weights a, b give the handed weights W₋ = 2a/3 + b/6 and W₊ = b/2; the Smith point is ¾(Γ(b/a) − 1/3), zero at b/a = 2 (the first rung ceiling). Lithium sits almost on it; gold far from it. Flipping M swaps the two weights and the sign.</td></tr>'
'<tr><td>VOTKP Mixer v2</td><td>Same script as the copy already on record; not re-read.</td></tr></table>')
A('<h2>4. What this does to the balls mixer v1</h2><ul>'
'<li>Its scene 5 repeats the Commutator Eye with less in it (no caps, no residues, no doubled steps), and counts the turn the long way round.</li>'
'<li>Its scenes 2 to 4 use separate spheres in flat space. The gap there, 2/(zⁿ + 1), is the coordinate distance from a cut level to the rim. It is exact, but it is not the layer between neighbours; that layer is δ.</li>'
'<li>Its lattice scene (simple, body-centred and face-centred cubic from 6, 8, 12 arms) is the flat limit z → 1 of the one-ball picture, where α → 90°. In the ball itself the six-arm tree closes only at the steps of section 2.</li>'
'<li>Nothing in v1 is wrong as arithmetic. It answers the question in a frame that is not the one his papers use.</li></ul>')
A('<h2>5. To talk about</h2><ol>'
'<li><b>Which frame is the crystal in.</b> One ball with overlap angle α and gap δ (his papers), or separate spheres (v1). The first gives closing steps that are not whole numbers; the second gives the ordinary cubic lattices but only as the flat limit.</li>'
'<li><b>What n means for matter.</b> n balls round an edge: 4 is flat space, 5 and 6 are the first two curved crystals, the wall is n → ∞. Whether an element\'s step picks an n is <span class="r">open</span>; z = 3 (the 2/3 line of the space step) gives α = 70.53°, the angle whose cosine is 1/3, and is not a closing step.</li>'
'<li><b>Radiating and receiving.</b> The bridge page\'s two handed weights are a pair from his own work that changes sign when flipped. That is a better candidate than the bare sign of Γ used in v1. <span class="r">Untested.</span></li>'
'<li><b>Eight and twelve arms.</b> Everything in section 2 is the six-arm tree. The eight-way form at 7 and the face-diagonal set have other wall angles (arms at 70.53° and 60°); <span class="r">not run</span>.</li>'
'<li><b>The knot side.</b> The same square and the same line decide when a knot\'s two meridians turn (Theorem 8.6). Whether a knot\'s commutator angle lands on 360°/n at the points that matter is <span class="r">not run</span>; the census limits 2cos(jπ/n)/m in 8.8 point that way.</li>'
'<li><b>Lean.</b> C1 and C2 are algebra in c = cosh, s = sinh and would sit beside J4 in BallJoins. <span class="r">Not written.</span></li></ol>')
